+### Several jobs on one slice (experimental)
+
+Two independent jobs, such as an RL trainer and an inference engine, can share
+one TPU slice and still run their own `torch.distributed` worlds. Every process
+starts the TPU runtime for the whole slice (`RANK` and `WORLD_SIZE` of the slice
+and the slice's `TORCH_TPU_SLICEBUILDER_ADDRESSES`), and sets
+`TORCH_TPU_JOB_DEVICE_IDS` to the global device ids of its job's processes, in
+job-rank order (for example `"0,1,2,3"` in one job and `"4,5,6,7"` in the
+other).
+
+1.  Every process of the slice first creates one slice-sized `tpu_dist`
+    backend, `slice_pg`, over a store shared by the slice (with the backend
+    constructor that TorchTPU registers for `tpu_dist`). It defines the slice's
+    ranks and carries transfers between the jobs.
+
+2.  Each job then initializes its own default group with its own ranks:
+    `dist.init_process_group(backend="tpu_dist", store=job_store,
+    rank=job_rank, world_size=job_size)`.
+
+Groups smaller than the slice, including each job's default group, are formed
+among the job's processes only, so each job creates its groups independently of
+the other. As in a single job, every process of a job creates every group of a
+layout, and a layout partitions the job. A group that mixes the jobs' processes
+must span the whole slice. To send tensors between the jobs, call
+`slice_pg.send([tensor], dst_slice_rank, tag)` and `slice_pg.recv(...)`.
+
+Limits: slice-sized groups other than the first, including any collective on
+`slice_pg`, need every process of both jobs, in the same order. With
+`TORCH_TPU_JOB_DEVICE_IDS` set, TorchTPU does not use the node-local store to
+speed up group creation.
+
